analysis:
The only change in this file is evictOldestEntry (head lines 1772-1779, base 1772-1787). The supplied units cover head lines 1-1255 and do not include the changed hunk, so it was judged from the patch excerpt alone.

Old behavior: the method iterated index.oldestEntries() and retired each victim through retireIndexedEntries. It returned 0 on an external change and counted an eviction on the first nonzero bytes freed.

New behavior: it adds a !isClosed guard and delegates to SSDOwnedEntryRetirement.evictOldest. That helper receives the index, an isClosed closure and the same per-tag retireIndexedEntries closure. The eviction stat is counted only when freed > 0.

Refusing removals on a closed cache is a tightening, not a regression. close() (lines 430-462) deregisters from diskBudget and clears staging, so a closed cache should not take part in budget eviction.

T-041 mitigations (b) and (c) depend on the helper preserving three behaviors. The helper's source was not supplied:
- skip a valid-but-undeletable victim so a newer one can still satisfy the box-wide budget;
- abort on externalChange;
- return the bytes freed for a single victim.

If the helper instead stops at the first failure, disk-budget enforcement could degrade. That is an availability and at-rest retention concern under the T-041 LRU budget. Integration should verify SSDOwnedEntryRetirement.evictOldest.

The unchanged surrounding code was examined and no regression was found in these areas:
- lookup ticket pinning (lines 511-584);
- endAdoption release (lines 616-652);
- donation gating on the adoption bound plus benefit floor (lines 883-888);
- the maxStageBytes persistence cap (lines 938-947);
- HMAC tags (lines 918-920);
- sidecar metadata, which carries only keyed tags and commitments (lines 1220-1232).
